                <pb facs="0394" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg388"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/5577: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-06-02T00:00:00-04:00">June 2,
                            1942—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2508. Your 2891 of May 23, 9 p.m. The Department’s views concerning the general
                    lines which the joint negotiations with the Swiss should follow in London were
                    set forth in its 2252 of May 20 Formulation of these views followed consultation
                    with the BEW and representatives of the British Embassy in Washington, as well
                    as consideration of the Embassy’s 2496 of May 8, 8 p.m. Within this agreed
                    general frame work latitude exists for the negotiators to pursue the strategy
                    proposed in the Embassy’s 2496 with the exception that the Department reserved
                    judgment on the specific suggestion regarding the release of strategic materials
                    for the Swiss army.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
